## Phase 0 — Ground rules and spec file

**Goal:** Give Claude Code a permanent memory of the project so every later prompt can be short.

```text
You are building a mobile app called "Court Shuffler" — a badminton session manager for casual group play.

Before writing any code, create two files in the project root:

1. CLAUDE.md — the persistent working agreement for this repo. It must contain:
   - Stack: Expo (managed workflow) + React Native + TypeScript (strict mode), expo-router for navigation, Zustand for state, Vitest for unit tests.
   - Hard constraint: NO persistence layer. No AsyncStorage, no SQLite, no backend, no auth, no network calls. All session state lives in memory and dies when the app is closed. This is intentional — a session is one afternoon of badminton.
   - Hard constraint: the shuffle/rotation logic must live in pure TypeScript modules under src/engine/ with zero React or React Native imports, so it can be unit tested in isolation.
   - Code conventions: functional components only, named exports, no default exports except expo-router screens, no `any`, colocate types in src/types.ts.
   - Rule: never introduce a new dependency without stating why in the commit message.

2. SPEC.md — the product spec. Write it from the following requirements, organised into sections (Session Setup, Rotation Fairness, Scoring, Mid-session Edits, End of Session):

   - The organiser sets up a session by answering questions on sequential screens, one topic per screen, in this order: (1) number of courts available, (2) session start time and end time, (3) average minutes per game, (4) scoring rules, (5) player names.
   - After setup, one primary button "Start shuffling" generates the first round.
   - A round = every available court filled with a doubles match (4 players). Players beyond capacity sit out that round and are first in line for the next one.
   - Fairness is the core promise: across the session, no player should have played meaningfully more games than any other. Target: the gap between the most-played and least-played active player never exceeds 1 game.
   - Variety matters too: avoid repeating the same partner or the same opponents until the engine has no better option.
   - Scores are entered per game (e.g. 21-15). Both players on a winning team receive identical win credit; individual totals then diverge as players are shuffled into new teams.
   - Players can be added or removed at ANY time during the session. A late joiner must slot into the rotation so that all active players get equal games FROM THAT POINT FORWARD — the late joiner must not monopolise courts to "catch up", and must not erase the points lead earned by players who arrived on time.
   - An "End session" action freezes the session and shows the top three players with their earned points, followed by a "Clear session" action that wipes everything for a fresh start.

Do not write any application code yet. Write only these two files, then show me SPEC.md and stop.
```

**Verify before continuing:** read `SPEC.md`. If it misstates any rule, fix it now with a follow-up message — every later phase reads from this file.

